  "notes": "The guitar-artist producer. Keys are a documented consequence: \"guitar keys (E, A, G, D) selected so the lead guitar can use open strings for Tele licks.\" The entry makes a point worth encoding about vocabulary breadth — \"his Darius Rucker productions are near-exclusively I–V–vi–IV, which shows he sets vocabulary per artist rather than per house style\" — so this model carries both poles as families: the I–IV–V-with-V7 guitar lane and the four-chord pop lane, rather than one house progression. Drums are arranged around the guitar and the entry states the constraint: \"conventional Nashville live 2&4, but arranged to stay out of the guitar's register — snare tuned high, cymbals restrained, kick short. Two-beat on the fast cuts, cross-stick on the ballads, very low fill density on the Rucker records (the groove is deliberately static),\" which is the low perc gain, the restrained hat fill and a long fill grid. The bass rule is quoted: \"live bass locked with the kick on 1 and 3 so the backbeat stays clear for guitar accents.\" His signature structural decision is the counter and the arrangement together: \"giving the lead guitar full 16- or 24-bar solo sections instead of 8-bar ones, and treating the guitar as a second lead voice.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high). ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc gain and the counter ratio.",
